`]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Why derisking is more than running a smaller model",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"To derisk a decision is to test it at small scale before a full run spends its compute on it. A small run helps only when it tests the same decision as the large one."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The playbook gives the compute breakdown of SmolLM3's pretraining. Ablations and debugging took 161,280 GPU-hours, including the compute spent on an unexpected scaling issue that forced a restart of training, and the main pretraining run took 276,480 GPU-hours ",e.jsx(n,{id:"smoltrainingplaybook-2025",children:"Ben Allal et al., 2025"}),". The two percentages answer different questions, because they divide by different amounts:"]}),e.jsx(t.figure,{"data-rehype-pretty-code-figure":"",children:e.jsx(t.pre,{tabIndex:"0","data-language":"text","data-theme":"github-dark-default",children:e.jsxs(t.code,{"data-language":"text","data-theme":"github-dark-default",style:{display:"grid"},children:[e.jsx(t.span,{"data-line":"",children:e.jsx(t.span,{children:"161280 / 276480 = 0.5833… = 58.3% of main-run compute"})}),`
`,e.jsx(t.span,{"data-line":"",children:e.jsx(t.span,{children:"161280 / (161280 + 276480) = 0.368421… = 36.8% of the two amounts combined"})})]})})}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The first divides by the main run alone: ablations and debugging cost 58% as much as the main run. The second divides by the sum of both amounts: 37% of the two together, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"36.84%"})," rounded to a whole number, went to ablations and debugging."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"When compute is scarce, spending more than a third of it on small runs can look wasteful. The SmolLM3 team spent that share anyway. We read it as the price of a main run whose decisions small runs had already checked."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A small result carries over to a larger run only if the larger run keeps what made the comparison informative: the optimizer, the peak learning rate, the global-batch schedule and the early-step behavior. Changing the context length changes the sequence length and the memory that attention needs. Changing the model scale can change how optimization behaves. A shift of domain changes the distribution the model learns. Estimate the metric noise instead of assuming it is negligible. Listing these limits does not settle whether a particular result transfers."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Package every result, a failure included, as a record you can reuse: the configuration, the code and data revisions, the seed plan, the logs, the metric outputs and the decision you took. A record of a negative result or a failed run can keep a later full-scale run from repeating it, and from relying on hindsight to judge what went wrong."})]})]})]}),`
