`]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Reading a measured next-token distribution",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The widget shows a base model's next-token distribution for one context at a time, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P(next token | prompt)"}),". This is the distribution that next-token training scores at that context. Each prompt button sets the context and with it the candidates, their percentages and the bars. The code prompt has the line ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"import torch"})," before ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"import torch.nn as"}),'. For each prompt the widget lists the most probable candidates, eight for the two sentence prompts and five for the code and arithmetic prompts. One of them, " ______" for the cat prompt, is a run of underscores, probably the blank that quiz-like documents leave to fill in. The printed percentages are absolute probabilities, and so are the bars: a bar across the full width would mean 100%. A confident prediction therefore shows one long bar, and a spread one shows only short bars, so you can compare prompts by the bar lengths alone.']}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The values were measured with SmolLM3-3B-Base ",e.jsx(n,{id:"smollm3-2025",children:"Bakouch et al., 2025"}),". The numbers hold for this model and its tokenizer."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:'Judge the uncertainty here by how much probability the top candidate leaves to the others. Two of the prompts are open English sentences, and they behave in opposite ways. The cat prompt is confident: " mat" gets about 84%, and no other listed candidate reaches 2%. "The cat sat on the mat" is a fixed phrase, and we read the 84% as the model having seen it many times in its training text. The color prompt is the most uncertain of the four. Its top candidate, " blue", gets only about 16%, followed by " green" (9.1%), " red" (8.0%), " purple" (4.6%) and " yellow" (4.2%). Each of these is a plausible way to finish the sentence, and the eight listed candidates together hold only about half of the probability. The rest lies on tokens outside the list.'}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['The other two prompts each have one clear winner. The code prompt gives " nn" more than 99%. After ',e.jsx(t.code,{children:"2+2="}),', "4" gets about 65%, but "5" still gets about 16%, and "2" and "3" get 3.6% and 3.2%. We read the 16% on "5" as the model having seen "2+2=5" in jokes and quotations: a base model continues a document the way its training text does, and nothing in that objective checks the arithmetic. Post-training can teach a model to answer a question instead of continuing a document (Weeks 12 and 13).']}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The same objective, with the same model, gives a confident distribution for one context and a spread one for another. What sets the uncertainty is the context, not the kind of text: an open sentence is not always uncertain."})]})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"Logits become a distribution over the vocabulary",accent:2,number:"28",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
