`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Why the factorization is an identity",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Formula (2) writes the probability of a whole sequence as a product of next-token probabilities. Take a realized sequence ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"x = (x_1, …, x_n)"}),". Here ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"x_t"})," is the token at position ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"t"}),", and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"x_{<t}"})," are the tokens before it, its prefix."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The derivation needs one rule. The ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"chain rule of probability"})," follows from the definition of conditional probability: for any two events, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P(A, B) = P(A) P(B | A)"}),". For three tokens, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P(x_1, x_2, x_3) = P(x_1, x_2) P(x_3 | x_1, x_2) = P(x_1) P(x_2 | x_1) P(x_3 | x_1, x_2)"}),": the first step takes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"A = (x_1, x_2)"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"B = x_3"}),", and the second applies the rule again to ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P(x_1, x_2)"}),". Each step splits off the last token and conditions it on the tokens before it, and for ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"n"})," tokens the steps end in the product of Formula (2). This product is called the ",e.jsx(t.strong,{children:"autoregressive factorization"}),". Multiplying its factors gives back the joint probability."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The factorization is an exact identity of probability, not an approximation or an optimization choice. The chain rule holds for every probability distribution over sequences, so the factorization assumes nothing about language. What a model approximates are the factors. It learns one next-token distribution for each position, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"p_θ(x_t | x_{<t})"}),", which Formula (1) computes from the logits with ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"t = i + 1"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The first factor, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P(x_1)"}),", has no token before it. By convention it is conditioned on an empty context, or on a special beginning-of-sequence token placed before the text, so every factor has the same form."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Each factor conditions on its prefix only: the prediction for position ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"t"})," may use ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"x_{<t}"}),", and nothing at or after position ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"t"}),". Week 2 builds the causal mask that enforces this rule inside the model."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The logarithm turns the product into a sum, because ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"log(ab) = log(a) + log(b)"}),". Negating both sides gives ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"-log P(x) = -∑_t log P(x_t | x_{<t})"}),". The negative log-probability of a sequence is therefore a sum of per-token penalties, one for each position."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"Training minimizes cross-entropy",accent:4,number:"30",children:[e.jsx(l,{math:"\\mathcal{L} = -\\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{(b,i)\\in\\mathcal{M}} \\log p_\\theta\\!\\left(y_{b,i}\\mid x_{b,\\le i}\\right)",label:"(3)"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
